Here are the answers:

1. **One thousand (1,000)** — You would have to count to "one thousand" before using the letter "A" in the English spelling of a whole number. (Numbers like "one," "two," ... "nine hundred ninety-nine" contain no "A"; "one thousand" is the first to include it.)

2. **"The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog."** — It is famous as a **pangram**: a sentence that contains every letter of the English alphabet at least once. It's commonly used to test typewriters, fonts, and keyboards.

3. **Story: The Three Little Pigs Meet Little Red Riding Hood**

Once upon a time, the three little pigs — Buster, Squeaky, and Hamlet — had just finished building their houses (one of straw, one of sticks, and one of bricks) when they heard a knock at the brick house door. They peeked out and saw a little girl in a red hood, carrying a basket of cookies.

"Hello!" said Little Red Riding Hood. "I'm on my way to Grandma's house, but I think I took a wrong turn at the forest."

The pigs looked at each other nervously. "You're not... you're not going to huff and puff, are you?" asked Hamlet.

Little Red Riding Hood giggled. "Of course not! I'm just lost. But I did meet a wolf on the way — he said he was going to Grandma's house too."

The three pigs gasped. "That wolf is a trickster!" cried Buster. "He tried to blow our houses down last week!"

So the three little pigs and Little Red Riding Hood hatched a plan. They hurried to Grandma's house, where they found the wolf already dressed in Grandma's nightgown. The pigs surrounded the house — Buster at the front door, Squeaky at the back, and Hamlet on the roof. Little Red Riding Hood knocked politely.

"Grandma, what big ears you have!" she said.

"The better to hear you with, my dear," growled the wolf.

"But Grandma, what big teeth you have!"

At that moment, Hamlet slid down the chimney and landed right on the wolf's head. Buster burst through the front door, and Squeaky blocked the back. The wolf yelped, jumped out the window, and ran all the way to the next county, never to be seen again.

Grandma came out of the closet, and everyone sat down to a feast of cookies and apple pie. Little Red Riding Hood promised to visit the pigs every Sunday, and the three little pigs finally lived happily — and safely — ever after.

**The End.** [end of text]


